Super Productivity, patched so a per-file compiler can run it. Two kinds of change:

1. `export type` on 7 re-export statements in 5 files, written by TypeScript's own quick fix for
   its isolatedModules errors (TS1205), via bench/real/isolated-modules-fix.mjs. A per-file
   compiler, which Vite is, can't tell that `export {SomeInterface} from './x'` names a type,
   compiles it to a runtime re-export of nothing, and the browser refuses to link the module.
   `ng build` emits with the whole program's types and never hits this. `ng new` has set
   `isolatedModules: true` since Angular 19, which would have caught these as they were written.

2. Two casts in datetime-picker.component.ts, so TypeScript 7 accepts it. vite-plugin-ngp
   type-checks with TypeScript 7 (tsgo), and a `vite build` fails on type errors. TypeScript 7
   infers `cal.activeDate` as `unknown` here (TS18046, and TS2769 at the second use), where
   TypeScript 6, which `ng build` uses, infers `Date`. The casts say what TypeScript 6 already
   inferred, and change nothing at runtime.

Both toolchains compile the patched files; `ng build` and `ng serve` measure the same sources.

